An integer literal is sixty-four bits wide.

WHAT THIS CLOSES. patches/m2libc/0005 put the 64-bit immediate vocabulary into
the M1 tables and said, in its own last paragraph, "nothing emits these yet".
This is the thing that emits them, and the parse in front of it, because the
truncation was never only in the emitter:

    M2libc strtoint()   returns int AND sign-extends from bit 31 by hand
    M2libc int2str()    masks to 32 bits
    write_load_immediate()  takes int, and the 32-bit forms are all it has

Built by gcc, where int is four bytes, micro-c could not REPRESENT a literal
wider than 32 bits in the source it compiles. `0x100000000` was 0.
`0x7fffffffffffffff` was `0xffffffff`. Nothing warned.

WHY IT IS NOT A CORNER CASE. tcc's parse_number tests

    if (n >= 0x8000000000000000ULL)

That literal was 0, so the test read `n >= 0` -- true for everything -- and the
same statement marked the constant UNSIGNED. Every constant the tcc we build
read was mis-typed, and it said so on every one of them:

    <command line>:27: warning: integer constant overflow

That warning is gone.

WHAT IT DOES

  cc_strings.c   strtolong()  the same reader, 64-bit accumulator, no
                              truncation step. Stops at the first character
                              that is not a digit in the current base, which is
                              how `0x8000000000000000ULL` loses its suffix
                              without a suffix table: char2hex('U') is -1.
                 long2str()   the printing half, mask built by arithmetic
                              rather than written as a literal -- see below
                 immediate_low_word / immediate_high_word

  cc_emit.c      write_load_immediate / emit_load_immediate take `long long`,
                 and above the threshold emit

                     aarch64   ldr_x<r>,8 / b_12 / %lo / %hi
                     amd64     movabs_<r>, %lo %hi

                 exactly the sequences tools/verify-imm64.sh proves by running
                 them and CI byte-anchors against real `as`.

  cc_core.c      primary_expr_number, the global-constant load, and the whole
                 constant_expression chain read through strtolong. An
                 eight-byte global initialiser gets its REAL high word instead
                 of a sign guess.

`long long` rather than `long`: M2-Planet maps both to register_size, but a
host `long` is 32 bits under LLP64, and the entire point of this patch is that
nothing depends on the host's integer width.

THE THRESHOLD IS 2^31, NOT 2^32, AND THAT IS THE INTERESTING PART.

The obvious rule is "wide if it does not fit 32 bits". It is wrong, because the
two targets are wrong in OPPOSITE DIRECTIONS in the range between:

    aarch64   ldr_w<r>,8 loads a WORD and ZERO-extends.
              Correct for 0..0xFFFFFFFF, wrong for every negative value.
    amd64     mov_<r>, %imm32 is MOV r/m64, imm32 and SIGN-extends.
              Correct for -0x80000000..0x7FFFFFFF, wrong above it.

The overlap is 0..0x7FFFFFFF and nothing else. One compile of libtcc.c emitted
189 constants in the disputed range -- literals like `0x80000000`, which
strtoint sign-extended to -2147483648, which aarch64 then zero-extended back
onto the right answer and amd64 sign-extended to 0xFFFFFFFF80000000. Right on
one architecture, wrong on the other, for four rounds, invisible to a suite run
on one column.

That is the sixth instance of the class MICRO-C.md counts, and the FIRST one
running the other way: here amd64 is wrong and aarch64 is right. The rule this
file has been carrying -- "a green amd64 difftest is not a result" -- needs its
mirror image beside it. Neither column is the reference. Case 54.

A SILENT TRUNCATION THAT COULD NOT BE FIXED, MADE LOUD. knight, x86, armv7l
and riscv32 have no 64-bit register, and riscv64's long-immediate lowering here
is a 30-bit split that has never been asked for more. On those targets the
value is still truncated, exactly as before. What is fixable is that it
happened silently, and it no longer does: emit_load_immediate names the
constant and the architecture on stderr. No emitted byte changes, so the guard
below is unaffected.

AND A TRAP THAT WAS ALREADY SET. int2str's unsigned path ends with

    i = x & (0x7FFFFFFF + 0x80000000);

which is a 32-bit mask ONLY IF the compiler that built it reads 0x80000000 as a
positive number. gcc does; M2-Planet, whose strtoint sign-extends bit 31, reads
it as -2147483648, so that expression is -1 and the mask does nothing. int2str
therefore truncates or does not truncate depending on WHICH COMPILER BUILT IT
-- and, after this patch, on whether that compiler had this patch. Splitting a
64-bit constant with it would have worked here and quietly stopped working at
self-compilation. long2str builds its mask by arithmetic instead. This is the
fifth time in this series that a fix landed somewhere nothing on the path was
reading, or read something that was not what it looked like.

HOW IT IS GUARDED, WHICH IS THE PART THAT MATTERS.

The new path is UNREACHABLE below the threshold, so anything that moves down
there is a bug in the widening rather than in the feature. So: compile the
whole corpus with the compiler as it was before this patch and with it after,
on both architectures, and require the emitted .M1 to be byte-identical
everywhere except the cases that deliberately carry a wide constant.

    54 cases x 2 architectures = 108 compilations
    104 byte-identical
      4 differ: cases 53 and 54, on both columns, and nothing else

tools/imm-identity.sh is that check, and local-build.sh runs it.

Over the whole tcc translation unit -- 369,433 lines of M1 -- the diff is
1,135 lines removed and 1,428 added, and EVERY ONE of them is a constant
outside 0..0x7FFFFFFF. The census closes exactly:

    removed   543 mov_x<r>,N          constants that had fitted the short form
              189 ldr_w / b_8 / %-N   the disputed-range literals
               17 mov_x<r>,-N
                8 global initialisers with a guessed high word
    added     523 mov_x<r>,N          (37 constants left the short form)
              219 ldr_x / b_12 / %lo %hi
                7 ldr_w / b_8         (narrow after being read correctly)
              226 b_*                 = 219 + 7, which is the arithmetic
                8 global initialisers with a real high word

Not one other line of 369,433 moved.

WHAT IT DOES NOT DO. It does not move the marker trail. The fault is still at
P151 -- next() returning into tccgen, outside the instrumented set -- and the
count went 41,852 to 41,790. Saying so plainly, because the last time this
series recorded a cause it was wrong for four rounds and the thing that settled
it was re-running the trail and finding it had not moved. This patch removes a
CONFOUND: MICRO-C.md said "fix the literals first or risk explaining a
symptom", and that is all that has been done. The fault is now worth diagnosing
on its own terms.

One new observation, undiagnosed: the `<command line>:27` warning changed from
"integer constant overflow" to "extra tokens after directive". Same line, a
different complaint, and it is NOT yet known whether that is tcc reading a
directive correctly for the first time or a second bug that the first one was
masking. It is the next cheap thing to look at.

